## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by reference — a new reference every render means a new prop, even if the content is identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|------|-----------------|----------|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child is pure and receives stable props |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Computation is expensive or result is passed as a prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

Key rule: `useMemo`/`useCallback` only pay off when the consumer also skips work (e.g., wrapped in `React.memo`). Memoizing in isolation just adds overhead.

```jsx
const expensiveResult = useMemo(() => compute(data), [data]);  // good
const trivialResult   = useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b]);          // pointless overhead
```

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Any component that calls `useContext` re-renders when the context value changes — including object identity changes.

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}

// GOOD: stable reference via useMemo
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return <UserContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</UserContext.Provider>;
}
```

Split contexts by update frequency — read-heavy data in one context, write callbacks in another, so readers don't re-render when callbacks change.

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every keystroke because `filter` and `onSelect` are recreated:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState([...largeDataset]);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)); // new array every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => selectItem(id);                // new function every render

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ items, onSelect }) { /* expensive render */ }
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when `query` or `items` actually change:

```jsx
function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState([...largeDataset]);

  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.name.includes(query)),
    [items, query]                                  // recomputes only when deps change
  );

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => selectItem(id), []); // stable reference

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* skips render when items and onSelect references are unchanged */
});
```

**The mental model**: `React.memo` is the gate; `useMemo`/`useCallback` make the props stable enough to pass through it. Without the gate, stabilizing props has no effect. Without stable props, the gate never closes.
